Abstract
This paper consolidates and audits an exploration beginning with Droncheff’s corre￾spondence matrices (CMs), which encode two-input Boolean predicates as binary 2 × 2
arrays. Clockwise rotation supplies a faithful 𝐶4 action on the four atomic CM features.
With an explicitly added cyclic-convolution product, these arrays form F2 [𝐶4]. XOR
cancellation, rotational correlations, and a conditional uniqueness theorem for normalized
Hamming measure are established. Fresh exhaustive checks of all 65,536 two-branch
operators confirm that global Hamming and full integer-correlation preservation leave only
32 phase-permutation operators. Thus these particular Boolean metrics do not provide a
Hadamard-like mixer.
An integral lift and the quotient identifying opposite phase directions produce Z[𝑖],
with the explicit intertwiner 𝑄𝑃 = 𝐽𝑄 connecting CM rotation to the real quarter-turn
matrix. The resulting phase-count representation reproduces conventional quantum gates
and extends to exact Clifford+𝑇 arithmetic. A separate, faithful compilation maps original
CM predicates to reversible oracles and diagonal phase gates. Proofs and new computations
distinguish this semantic bridge from a claim that ordinary CM truth-table operations
already constitute quantum mechanics. Related matrix logics, finite-field models, path-sum
calculi, cyclotomic synthesis, and model-counting methods substantially precede the
general ideas. Plausible contributions lie in typed CM-based explanation, certified local
rewrites, and structure-aware compilation; neither physical novelty nor a new simulation
speedup is established.
Status and provenance. An AI-assisted research draft prepared from the supplied manuscript and
this investigation, with independently rerun computational checks. It is not peer reviewed and is not
a claim of new physical laws. The original manuscript is attributed to its author; the present text is a
proposed extension and audit, not a silent revision of that manuscript. All numerical search summaries
are generated by the accompanying code. Literature review cutoff: 17 September 2026.
